</div>
<div>
<div class="faq-list"><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is the Tramontina Professional Fusion the same as the regular Tramontina Professional Restaurant pan?</summary><p class="faq-answer">No. They are closely related, but the Professional Fusion version has a rivetless interior that is easier to clean. The regular Professional Restaurant version is still a strong fallback if Fusion availability changes.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is this nonstick pan induction compatible?</summary><p class="faq-answer">No. This is not the right default choice for induction cooktops. Induction buyers should choose an induction-compatible nonstick pan with a magnetic, flat base.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>How long should a nonstick pan last?</summary><p class="faq-answer">A good nonstick pan should give solid everyday performance, but it should not be treated like lifetime cookware. Coatings eventually decline, especially with high heat, metal utensils, or dishwasher use.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is ceramic nonstick safer or better?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Ceramic nonstick can be the better choice for buyers who want PFAS-free cookware, but it is not automatically more durable. It still needs careful use and realistic expectations.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Should I buy an expensive nonstick pan?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Usually not. In nonstick, the coating is the limiting factor. A reliable, reasonably priced pan often makes more sense than a premium pan with a surface that still wears out.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Can I put this pan in the dishwasher?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Avoid it. Even if a listing says dishwasher-safe, hand washing is the better ownership decision for preserving the nonstick surface.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>What utensils should I use with nonstick?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Use silicone, nylon, or wood utensils. Metal utensils are one of the fastest ways to shorten the useful life of a nonstick pan.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>What size nonstick pan should most people buy?</summary><p class="faq-answer">A 10-inch pan is the best default for eggs, omelets, and everyday quick meals. A 12-inch pan makes more sense for families or larger batches.</p></details>
